Input: {"question": "When was the Croatian money introduced?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00", "start": 0, "end": 63, "text": "Croatia, they introduced the Independent State of Croatia kuna."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00", "start": 64, "end": 273, "text": "This currency remained in circulation until 1945, when it – along with competing issues by the communist Partisans – disappeared with the establishment of FPR Yugoslavia and was replaced by the Yugoslav dinar."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00", "start": 274, "end": 406, "text": "The modern kuna was introduced on May 30, 1994, starting a transitional period from the Croatian dinar, ending on December 31, 1994."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00", "start": 407, "end": 473, "text": "The exchange rate between dinar and kuna was 1 kuna = 1000 dinars."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00", "start": 474, "end": 570, "text": "Since then, Croatia has been in a system of de facto currency board with a fixed exchanged rate."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00", "start": 571, "end": 593, "text": "The kuna was pegged to"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01", "start": 0, "end": 152, "text": "Independent State of Croatia kuna The kuna was the currency of the Independent State of Croatia in the period between 1941 and 1945 during World War II."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01", "start": 153, "end": 261, "text": "The word \"kuna\" means \"marten\" in Croatian and the same word is used for the current Croatian kuna currency."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01", "start": 262, "end": 305, "text": "This kuna was subdivided into 100 \"banica\"."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01", "start": 306, "end": 357, "text": "It was preceded and replaced by the Yugoslav dinar."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01", "start": 358, "end": 449, "text": "The Croatian kuna was introduced in the Independent State of Croatia (NDH) on 26 July 1941."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01", "start": 450, "end": 573, "text": "The kuna replaced the Yugoslav dinar at par and was fixed to the German Reichsmark (ℛℳ) with dual exchange rates, one fixed"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02", "start": 0, "end": 20, "text": "the Yugoslav dinar ."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02", "start": 21, "end": 255, "text": "[ 9 ] A 5000 Independent State of Croatia kuna banknote , 1943 series From 1941–1945 during World War II in Yugoslavia , the Independent State of Croatia officially adopted the Kuna as the currency for the first time on 26 July 1941 ."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02", "start": 256, "end": 373, "text": "The Independent State of Croatia kuna at the time of adoption included banknotes of 10 , 50 , 100 , 500 , and 1,000 ."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02", "start": 374, "end": 467, "text": "Banknotes of 1 , 2 , were later introduced in 1942 , and 5,000 banknotes were added in 1943 ."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02", "start": 468, "end": 496, "text": "[ 10 ] The Kuna started with"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03", "start": 0, "end": 64, "text": "introduced in the Independent State of Croatia on 26 July 1941 ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03", "start": 65, "end": 205, "text": "Italian lira and German Reichsmark were printed parallel to the kuna and in occupied countries , but were not legal tender in Nazi Germany ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03", "start": 206, "end": 490, "text": "[ 4 ] The kuna replaced the Yugoslav dinar at par and was fixed to the Reichsmark with dual exchange rates , [ citation needed ] one fixed at 20 kuna = 1 RM , the other a state-stimulated rate of : 31 December 1941 - 25.00 Kn = 1 ℛℳ 31 December 1942 - 37.50 Kn = 1 ℛℳ 31 December 1943"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "May 30, 1994", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C1", "answer": "26 July 1941", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01:c0", "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02:c0", "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00:c0", "entity": "Croatian money", "scope": "introduction date", "claim": "The modern kuna was introduced on May 30, 1994.", "answer": "May 30, 1994", "quote": "The modern kuna was introduced on May 30, 1994, starting a transitional period from the Croatian dinar, ending on December 31, 1994.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01", "card_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01:c0", "entity": "Croatian money", "scope": "introduction date", "claim": "The Croatian kuna was introduced in the Independent State of Croatia on 26 July 1941.", "answer": "26 July 1941", "quote": "The Croatian kuna was introduced in the Independent State of Croatia (NDH) on 26 July 1941.", "original_document_number": 2}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02", "card_id": "ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02:c0", "entity": "Croatian money", "scope": "first introduction", "claim": "The Croatian money (Kuna) was first introduced on 26 July 1941 by the Independent State of Croatia during World War II.", "answer": "26 July 1941", "quote": "[ 9 ] A 5000 Independent State of Croatia kuna banknote , 1943 series From 1941–19